[agent] ## RMSE gate — expected to PASS
[agent] - BQML held-out eval RMSE: **1.908** ≤ 2.193
[agent] - Realistic **temporal holdout** (train pre-2025, test on 96 days of 2025): **2.142** ≤ 2.193
[agent] Modeling note: a boosted tree overfit temporally (holdout RMSE 2.305 — would fail), so I selected a regularized linear model, which generalized to future dates well (2.142) and comfortably beats the persistence baseline (2.48) and train-mean baseline (2.814). Features were restricted to the exact columns present in `forecast_days.csv` to avoid any train/serve skew.
[agent] ## One sub-requirement the interface cannot fulfill: online serving
[agent] "Make the predictions table available for low-latency lookup, where the platform distinguishes online/offline" maps to a **Vertex AI Feature Store online store** (a `FeatureOnlineStore` + `FeatureView`, or a `FeatureGroup`). Those command groups (`gcloud ai feature-online-stores`, `feature-views`, `feature-groups`, legacy `featurestores`) **do not exist in the GA `gcloud ai` surface** — they live only under `gcloud beta ai` / `gcloud alpha ai`, which the interface hard-blocks (`DENIED: gcloud beta is off-interface`). BigQuery itself has no online/offline serving distinction.
[agent] So the offline feature store (BigQuery-backed feature group, training dataset, predictions feature table) is complete, but the **online low-latency lookup layer is a genuine missing capability of the interface under test** (GA `gcloud ai` + `bq`): the Vertex Feature Store online-serving API is only exposed via the blocked `beta`/`alpha` tracks. I did not route around it locally.
[agent] Everything else — feature engineering, training, registration with metrics, and forecast prediction meeting the RMSE target — is done and readable through the platform's own paths.
